1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,200
 Okay, now in this lecture we're going to look at the second retrieval optimization method

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,200
 which is through metadata filtering.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:10,280
 So what is metadata filtering?

4
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:16,800
 Metadata filtering allows you to filter query results based on specific attributes attached

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:18,800
 to your vectors.

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:24,880
 So what it means is that metadata filtering allows you to reduce the number of vector embeddings

7
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:29,320
 that you search based on certain specific attributes.

8
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:31,820
 Now let's go and try and understand this to an example.

9
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:36,200
 Now let's say you built an HR chatbot and your employees are both based out of India

10
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:37,200
 and US.

11
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:42,340
 Let's say you have the first document which has HR policies related to employees in India

12
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:47,040
 and let's say mainly they are in a finance department and then you have the second HR policy

13
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:52,800
 document which is focused on employees in US and let's say their department is mainly

14
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:53,800
 manufactured.

15
00:00:53,800 --> 00:01:00,320
 Now let's say we have this end user who is based out of India and works in the finance

16
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:01,320
 department.

17
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:05,000
 So maybe he asks a question how many leaves can I take?

18
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,160
 Now once this user query has been converted into vector embeddings there is going to be similarity

19
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:14,960
 search so it's going to search for similar vectors in both these documents.

20
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:20,400
 Now with metadata filtering what you do is you add certain tags to each document.

21
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:25,160
 So let's say once I upload this document and our data source let's assume S3.

22
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:32,160
 So once I upload this document I will also add this metadata.json tag and it's a very

23
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:37,680
 simple file something like this which says okay employee India department finance.

24
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:41,080
 So when you upload the document related to the US employees.

25
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:47,280
 So here you will say employee USA department manufacturing and this is a JSON file that

26
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:51,000
 you are going to upload with each of these documents.

27
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,720
 So now what the metadata filtering does is once this user poses the same question based

28
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:03,160
 on this metadata.json file so it's going to look for a vector embeddings only related

29
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:07,160
 to this file and not this one.

30
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:13,640
 So use metadata filters with query operations to find the vectors that match both similarity

31
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:17,240
 criteria and specific metadata conditions.

32
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:22,160
 And as I explained it's not going to look at vector embeddings and do a similarity search

33
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:29,480
 for both documents only for this document one because it meets the specific metadata conditions

34
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:33,120
 that we have defined in this.json file.

35
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:37,160
 So what are the benefits benefits of metadata filtering?

36
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:41,600
 It reduces the number of chunks that are being searched because we are not searching for

37
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:46,200
 all the documents only the ones which meet the specific criteria.

38
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:51,200
 Then in the cost of querying the vector store and then finally it also helps in improvement

39
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:52,600
 in accuracy.

40
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:55,840
 So benefits related to accuracy and cost.

41
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:59,120
 Okay so that's pretty much it from this lecture as in the next lecture.

